WEBVTT

00:00:00.685 --> 00:00:24.165
<v Casey>Hey there, good to have you. Tuesdays on Cariboo Signals keep coming back to forestry and timber, and today's story is Quesnel. Millar Western's pulp mill curtailment is now indefinite, about 90 workers are affected, and the city is formally asking the province for Community Transition Services on November 16th. Then the big question: who actually counts as affected when a mill goes quiet? Stay with us.

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:55.624
<v Casey>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities. I'm Casey. It's Tuesday, November 17th, 2026. Today's theme is Working Lands &amp; Industry. Reaching you from the heart of the Cariboo, the homeland of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations.

00:00:56.624 --> 00:01:30.270
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. Farms, mills and mines are all running the same numbers this week, and today we look at what happens to the people around them when those numbers stop working. Out at Horsefly Lake this morning it's partly cloudy and around minus one, foggy through the day with a high of four. Across the Cariboo, 100 Mile House is around minus three and clear, Williams Lake is around minus two with fog, Quesnel is around minus two and clear, and Anahim Lake is around minus four with fog. Heads up if you're driving into Williams Lake this morning.

00:01:30.470 --> 00:01:31.494
<v Casey>Starting with the news.

00:01:32.894 --> 00:01:54.574
<v Casey>A lot of today's news is about what it takes to keep a farm, a forest or a mill running, and some of it lands right in our own backyard. Starting with the courts in Quesnel: a two-day trial for 25-year-old Farbn Japow wrapped up Friday. He faces drug trafficking, weapons, and police flight charges tied to an August 2024 incident, and the judge has reserved a decision.

00:01:56.157 --> 00:02:17.621
<v Riley>Subscribers to the 100 Mile Free Press, along with the Clearwater Times, the Barriere Star Journal and the Ashcroft-Cache Creek Journal, have a new subscription system. Black Press moved those websites to a platform called Admiral earlier this month. If your inbox has a sign-in request, it's legitimate, and publisher Martina Dopf said so directly.

00:02:18.071 --> 00:02:23.687
<v Casey>The page to skip is the one asking for credit card details. Existing subscribers have already paid.

00:02:25.359 --> 00:02:42.495
<v Casey>Near Green Lake, Aaron Burwell, a lifelong resident who runs Wilderness Custom Homes, got tired of illegal dumpsites growing in the backcountry he rides and hunts. Earlier this month he and his family cleaned one up. He says he's seen them everywhere he goes in the bush, and that the piles keep getting bigger.

00:02:43.992 --> 00:03:14.446
<v Riley>Quattro Farms, a joint venture of five family farms near Bow Island, Alberta, is trialling a multi-species cover crop ahead of its potatoes. Potato production involves heavy tillage, which leaves bare soil open to wind erosion over winter. The agronomist, Emily Ford, hopes the mix's different root structures will protect the soil without costing potato yield. The trial runs through the Farm Resilience Mentorship field trials program, and the operation's specialty crops also include mint, hemp and seed canola.

00:03:15.864 --> 00:03:36.656
<v Casey>Swedish farmers are reconsidering grain storage after a large 2025 harvest. Low grain prices and trouble getting crops delivered at harvest time are pushing some of them to hold grain and sell later. Lantmännen cites the Swedish Board of Agriculture's forecast of 5.9 million tonnes for the crop, and some farmers are planning storage into new machinery sheds.

00:03:38.112 --> 00:04:05.400
<v Riley>The Canadian Institute of Mining, Metallurgy and Petroleum says Canada's push to speed up mine development risks running ahead of its workforce. CIM president John Rhind told The Northern Miner that the will is there, but the ability to deliver is not quite yet. Recruiting younger workers and affordable electricity are the biggest hurdles, and he says there's only a year or two to get training and recruitment on track for the coming decade.

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:09.122
<v Casey>A year or two to build a training pipeline is a short clock.

00:04:10.942 --> 00:04:39.486
<v Casey>Ohio State University Extension guidance published November 15th says grazing leftover corn residue can help cattle and sheep conserve hay. For a field yielding 200 bushels an acre, the guidance estimates about 1,600 pounds of usable dry matter per acre, assuming half the residue is available. Strip grazing keeps animals away from downed grain, which can cause acidosis. The coverage puts winter feed at roughly 25 to 35 per cent of annual cow-calf production costs.

00:04:40.974 --> 00:05:10.990
<v Riley>A spring survey by the consultancy Ludvig &amp; Co found larger Swedish dairy farms planning to build or expand barns in 2026, while many smaller operations plan to close because weak profitability is squeezing them. The overall farm profitability index came in at plus 3, but crop growers sat at minus 54. A study of 326 dairy farms found that spreading production across different farm sizes could reduce exposure to disease, power cuts and floods.

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:19.080
<v Casey>Lisa Ekman at Örebro University argues that the value of small farms, in biodiversity and rural life, rarely shows up in their accounts.

00:05:20.751 --> 00:05:43.551
<v Casey>KMC MAX showed its first physical KMC MAX 100 forestry mulcher at Uruforest in Uruguay in October. It's a tracked machine built on the engineering of the FMC and Kootrac platforms, updated with hydrostatic drive and new operator cabins, and aimed at forestry and wildfire work. The unit on display was a non-operational prototype, so the public debut is a showing, not a machine doing work yet.

00:05:45.048 --> 00:06:13.240
<v Riley>EuroTier in Hannover ran last week, and the AI on display was mostly about spotting problems early. Cameras flag lameness and detect calving, and feeding robots adjust water in the mix when the base feed changes. Nedap says its lameness camera was validated on farms from 300 to more than 8,000 cows, and it's slated for selected European markets from early 2027. The systems flag changes, but they don't replace a vet.

00:06:14.746 --> 00:06:24.178
<v Casey>The federal budget commits $6.5 billion to forestry, but forestry groups say the sector is in what they call a deepening crisis, and that it needs more help than money.

00:06:25.775 --> 00:06:43.015
<v Riley>Benchmark Western Spruce-Pine-Fir 2x4 lumber fell 11 per cent in the week ending October 30th, to US$405 per thousand board feet. That's 7 per cent below a year earlier. The drop was uneven, and it kept going even as field inventories fell to very low levels.

00:06:43.465 --> 00:06:46.401
<v Casey>Low inventories should have held the price up, and they didn't.

00:06:48.216 --> 00:07:09.808
<v Casey>The USDA cut its 2026/27 U.S. corn yield estimate to 179.6 bushels an acre in its November 10th report, taking about 141 million bushels out of the projected crop. Soybean stocks tightened as well. December corn rose nearly 12 cents to $4.51 a bushel on the news. Iowa's estimate held at a record 219 bushels an acre.

00:07:11.349 --> 00:07:32.093
<v Riley>The Rural Municipalities of Alberta released a report called Growing Success, with six recommendations to help farms add agritourism. They target liability, municipal approvals, marketing and access to information. The model is a working farm where agriculture stays the main business while visitors come for U-picks, tours and farm stays.

00:07:33.518 --> 00:07:52.294
<v Casey>ADP payroll data shows pay for job-stayers in natural resources and mining rose 4.1 per cent year over year in October. The national figure was 4.5 per cent. Statistics Canada puts average weekly earnings in mining, quarrying and oil and gas at $2,584 in August, against $1,355 across all industries.

00:07:53.729 --> 00:08:18.393
<v Riley>Copa-Cogeca, the umbrella group for European farm organizations, asked the European Commission to exempt agriculture and food production from energy restrictions during any shortage. Its letter went out ahead of an October 29th meeting of EU energy ministers. The group also wants rural grid connections, simpler permits and financing so farms can produce their own biogas and solar power.

00:08:19.904 --> 00:08:49.792
<v Casey>A strong 2026 to 27 El Niño is disrupting food production. Peru has declared an emergency across more than 70 per cent of its municipalities over flood danger, and warns that more than 9 million people face a high risk of floods and landslides. In the Philippines, unmilled rice production fell 18 per cent in the three months to September. Kellanova has changed the ingredients in Rice Krispies and Coco Pops sold in the UK and Europe, citing weather-related problems with rice and grain supply.

00:08:50.242 --> 00:08:53.458
<v Riley>The grocery aisle is where the bill for that shows up first.

00:08:55.133 --> 00:09:16.021
<v Riley>Canadian farmers reporting their current financial health at 64 per cent good or excellent in the October Canadian Farmer Sentiment Index, which is an all-time high for that question since April 2025. The index is based on 491 producers. High input costs and market swings still keep short-term optimism in check.

00:09:17.371 --> 00:09:28.107
<v Casey>Between falling lumber prices and forestry groups calling the sector a deepening crisis, the thing to watch is what happens around a mill when it goes quiet. That's where Quesnel stands today.

00:09:29.507 --> 00:09:50.747
<v Riley>The Williams Lake Stampede Association is a nonprofit that runs the world-famous Williams Lake Stampede on Canada Day weekend. If you've never been, the Stampede is one of the biggest weekends on the regional calendar, and it's the nonprofit that keeps it running from one year to the next. You can find the details at williamslakestampede.com.

00:09:51.348 --> 00:09:55.556
<v Casey>Pulling off an event that size takes a lot of planning nobody sees until the gates open.

00:09:56.245 --> 00:10:00.797
<v Riley>From the rodeo grounds to a mill gate, the deep dive picks up where the news left off.

00:10:02.197 --> 00:10:23.757
<v Riley>Millar Western's Quesnel River Pulp curtailment became indefinite last week, and we covered the company's side on November 10th. Today the question is who counts as affected. The company's decision touches about 90 workers. The City of Quesnel's release names a much wider group, and a town defining its own list is the right place to start.

00:10:24.361 --> 00:10:32.097
<v Casey>Ninety people on a payroll is one count. Employees, contractors, families, local businesses and the economy as a whole is a different count.

00:10:32.776 --> 00:10:38.480
<v Riley>The word that matters is curtailment. Millar Western chose it on purpose, and it's not a closure.

00:10:39.034 --> 00:10:49.810
<v Casey>It matters for who's on the hook. A closure ends contracts. A curtailment leaves them hanging, and the effluent system we mentioned on the 10th keeps running under the company's contract.

00:10:50.337 --> 00:10:56.689
<v Riley>That's a sign the company expects a restart. Nobody keeps a treatment system running for a mill they've written off.

00:10:57.319 --> 00:11:02.919
<v Casey>Or they're keeping it running because the contract says so. The contract is the reason, not the optimism.

00:11:03.529 --> 00:11:10.473
<v Riley>The contract still means someone is paid to keep that system working with no pulp coming through the mill. Those are jobs.

00:11:11.117 --> 00:11:18.317
<v Casey>The sources don't say who staffs it. If it's contractors, they're on the list already, and they're the group the release names second-to-last.

00:11:18.645 --> 00:11:21.493
<v Riley>Second-to-last is a sentence order, not a ranking.

00:11:21.807 --> 00:11:23.759
<v Casey>Ranking is where lines get drawn, though.

00:11:24.411 --> 00:11:36.515
<v Riley>Scale first. The mill is rated at 375,000 tonnes a year of softwood pulp. That's about a thousand tonnes a day, and the company says 93 per cent of sales go to Asian markets, mainly China.

00:11:37.089 --> 00:11:44.065
<v Casey>So the town is exposed to two decisions it can't see. One is the company's. The other is a buyer in China who isn't in the room.

00:11:44.730 --> 00:11:52.738
<v Riley>Which is why the tariffs matter more than the curtailment wording. The company cited weak demand, poor pricing and tariffs when it started this.

00:11:53.406 --> 00:12:01.606
<v Casey>And the buyer's side doesn't show up in any list Quesnel can write. Nine of every ten sales dollars go to markets nobody in Quesnel negotiates with.

00:12:02.253 --> 00:12:12.357
<v Riley>Then the mill's economy is still local even if the sales aren't. Quesnel is a city of about 9,000 people, and 90 workers is roughly one resident in every hundred.

00:12:13.014 --> 00:12:22.582
<v Casey>Visible to the people who live there, yes. Look at the mayor's statement. Coralee Oakes put employees first, then contractors, families and local businesses, and the economy came last.

00:12:23.222 --> 00:12:29.646
<v Riley>She named all four groups in one breath. Most companies can't manage that in their first announcement.

00:12:30.264 --> 00:12:40.952
<v Casey>Now look at what's missing. The release doesn't name any First Nation among the groups it lists, and the Observer doesn't either. I'd want to know whether any are in the circle, and who asked.

00:12:41.592 --> 00:12:48.544
<v Riley>A city list reflects what city hall can see, and some workforces and businesses don't show up in municipal records.

00:12:49.115 --> 00:12:56.515
<v Casey>What the city can't see is the first thing that gets dropped. Nobody left off a list complains until the services are already designed.

00:12:57.088 --> 00:13:02.576
<v Riley>Then the list needs an owner who can widen it later. That's a process problem, and it's fixable.

00:13:02.904 --> 00:13:06.640
<v Casey>It's fixable if someone names the owner. Right now the release doesn't.

00:13:07.288 --> 00:13:22.328
<v Riley>The tax base is why the economy belongs on the list at all. Heavy industry accounts for 53 per cent of Quesnel's tax base, according to the mayor. The city is also losing a $150,000 BC Hydro revenue grant tied to the mill's electricity use.

00:13:22.932 --> 00:13:34.756
<v Casey>Fifty-three per cent in one industry is a concentration problem. Retraining one worker doesn't refill a tax roll, and a $150,000 grant is a line in a budget that a council has to fill somewhere else.

00:13:35.305 --> 00:13:42.089
<v Riley>So the city's economic-health language earns its place. The council is protecting the revenue as well as the people.

00:13:42.768 --> 00:13:53.256
<v Casey>Council has a reason to count every business that pays the bills, and a reason to count the tax more carefully than the people paid in cash. The release doesn't say which one drove the order.

00:13:53.871 --> 00:14:15.999
<v Riley>Now the provincial side. The B.C. government's forestry support page lists Community Transition Services as on-the-ground support after an economic disruption, quote, "such as a mill closure." Then WorkBC, the B.C. Employer Training Grant, the Community Workforce Response Grant and REDIP. That's a real toolkit, more developed than what a town had during past closures.

00:14:16.667 --> 00:14:31.555
<v Casey>Such as a mill closure. Earlier documentation says the program is meant to switch on when a closure is announced. It describes needs assessments, job fairs and town halls. An indefinite curtailment with a restart that depends on fibre and prices is not a closure.

00:14:32.070 --> 00:14:40.198
<v Riley>The page says "such as," so it's an example, not a limit. A mill idled for an unknown stretch is an economic disruption in plain terms.

00:14:40.789 --> 00:14:45.885
<v Casey>Plain terms aren't eligibility rules. Someone has to decide that, and the page doesn't say who.

00:14:46.405 --> 00:15:06.757
<v Riley>Then look at what Quesnel did. My Cariboo Now reports the city is formally requesting Community Transition Services from the province on November 16th. The Observer says the release raised concerns about workers, contractors, families and local businesses. That's Quesnel setting the terms, rather than waiting for a plan to arrive.

00:15:07.270 --> 00:15:14.798
<v Casey>It's a request. A request is not a commitment. Neither article says the province has agreed, and neither says who decides who qualifies.

00:15:15.448 --> 00:15:24.480
<v Riley>The request is on paper with a date and a list of groups. The province now has to answer it in writing, which is more than a town gets from silence.

00:15:24.796 --> 00:15:28.172
<v Casey>The province's answer is what counts, and nobody's reported one yet.

00:15:28.758 --> 00:15:48.758
<v Riley>The tools are real, though. Atlas Holdings bought the Quesnel and Slave Lake mills from West Fraser in 2024, and it has a long-term fibre supply agreement for this mill. Ottawa gave Millar Western a C$100 million Large Enterprise Tariff Loan in July. So the capital exists. The question is whether it gets used for the whole circle.

00:15:49.327 --> 00:15:59.135
<v Casey>Those are agreements about the company. They tell you the mill has a supplier and a lender. They don't tell you who's on the list, and a lender's loan isn't a worker's paycheque.

00:15:59.793 --> 00:16:06.497
<v Riley>Which is a reason to make the list public. A wide list on paper is checkable later. Anyone can hold the city to it.

00:16:07.044 --> 00:16:14.340
<v Casey>Only if someone publishes the criteria. A list in a press release and a list that governs eligibility are two different documents.

00:16:14.619 --> 00:16:16.787
<v Riley>Then publish the criteria next to the list.

00:16:17.357 --> 00:16:23.133
<v Casey>And add a date. Who reviews the list in three months, and who gets added if a contractor shows up late?

00:16:23.421 --> 00:16:27.813
<v Riley>You're asking for the boring parts. I'll take them. They're where transition plans actually fail.

00:16:28.414 --> 00:16:37.030
<v Casey>They're also where the cost moves. Skip the needs assessment and you pay later, in a downtown that empties out or a family that leaves before anyone counts it.

00:16:37.670 --> 00:16:45.958
<v Riley>Contractors depend on the mill's schedule without being on its payroll. If the mill is idle for months, a contractor's trucks sit too.

00:16:46.550 --> 00:17:03.374
<v Casey>That's the outer ring I'd watch. Canfor said in July it would shut its Northwood pulp mill in Prince George, which has 300,000 tonnes of capacity. Domtar curtailed its Howe Sound mill in August. Three mills, three towns, and the same outer ring of businesses in the same position each time.

00:17:04.039 --> 00:17:13.367
<v Riley>That's a sector pattern, and it argues for a standard list. Every town could use the same definition of affected, so the province isn't rebuilding it for each case.

00:17:13.998 --> 00:17:24.374
<v Casey>A standard would help. But that's a province-level answer, and Quesnel still needs a local one. Right now the release names contractors and businesses, and nobody is named to answer for them.

00:17:25.036 --> 00:17:30.804
<v Riley>The circle is on paper now. That's a start, and it's more than most plans offer at the beginning.

00:17:31.368 --> 00:17:38.992
<v Casey>A start is a document. It isn't a service. Who's still on that list when the mill has been quiet for six months and the first job fair is over?

00:17:39.269 --> 00:17:46.389
<v Riley>The council is the body best placed to say who's in, because it sees the businesses, the tax and the families at once.

00:17:46.939 --> 00:17:56.451
<v Casey>Council also depends on that 53 per cent. That's the incentive, and it's the reason the list should have a second reader outside city hall who can check the outer ring.

00:17:57.088 --> 00:18:02.576
<v Riley>I'd want a published rule first. An outside reader without a rule is just a second opinion.

00:18:03.229 --> 00:18:08.965
<v Casey>A second opinion is better than none. It's how you find the business left off because its owner never came to council.

00:18:09.583 --> 00:18:16.919
<v Riley>I'd still hold the line on the rule. Publish the criteria, and then the outside reader has something to check against.

00:18:17.549 --> 00:18:23.101
<v Casey>The criteria come first, fine. The reader still matters. One document can't catch its own blind spots.

00:18:23.648 --> 00:18:34.224
<v Riley>Then it stays open. The city has written a wide circle, the province has a toolkit, and the company has a loan and a fibre contract. What's missing is the rule that ties them together.

00:18:34.791 --> 00:18:45.759
<v Casey>And who gets to read that rule once it exists. Does a curtailment count as a closure in the province's eyes, and can anyone outside city hall see the list? Nothing in these sources answers either.

00:18:46.401 --> 00:18:57.585
<v Riley>Both belong in writing from the people who hold the pen. If you have a story tip, or something from a mill town we missed, write to feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a. We read every one.

00:18:57.884 --> 00:19:01.924
<v Casey>I'll be watching for the province's answer to Quesnel. Have a good Tuesday.

00:19:02.290 --> 00:19:04.066
<v Riley>Mind the fog into Williams Lake.

00:19:04.666 --> 00:19:23.922
<v Riley>Cariboo Signals is produced by Erich Zirnhelt — scripts by Claude, today's voices by OpenAI TTS, theme by Suno. Weather data from Open-Meteo. Today's episode included additional web research via Brave Search and Kagi. Automated with GitHub Actions, hosted on Cloudflare Pages. Find us at cariboo signals dot c-a.